$ qube qb aging-reports list --help
Usage: qube qb aging-reports list <connection> [flags]

List aging reports.  GET /api/v2/connections/{connection_id}/aging-reports

qbXML: AgingReportQueryRq, from qbXML 2.0.

An aging report tracks outstanding invoices (in accounts receivable accounts) and unpaid bills
(in accounts payable accounts). Notice that you could use a collections report instead of the
aging query, if that better suited your needs.

Flags:
  --request-id STRING              Your own label for the request, sent to QuickBooks as its
                                   qbXML requestID and echoed in the raw answer (response_xml);
                                   optional, since the answer's webhook already carries the
                                   request's id.
  --aging-report-type STRING       (required) The type of aging report being queried.
                                   One of: APAgingDetail, APAgingSummary, ARAgingDetail,
                                   ARAgingSummary, CollectionsReport.
  --display-report                 Set DisplayReport to true to display this report within the
                                   QuickBooks UI.
  --include-accounts STRING        Indicates whether this report should include all accounts or
                                   just those that are currently in use.
                                   One of: All, InUse.
  --include-column STRING...       One of: Account, Aging, Amount, AmountDifference,
                                   AverageCost, BilledDate, BillingStatus, CalculatedAmount, …
                                   (75; --help --json lists them). (repeat the flag, or a JSON
                                   array)
  --report-account-filter OBJECT   Allows you to query for a specified account type or for
                                   specific list elements.
                                   JSON: {"account_type_filter", "full_name", …}. Exactly one
                                   of: account_type_filter | list_id | full_name |
                                   list_id_with_children | full_name_with_children.
  --report-aging-as-of STRING      The report will return aging information up to the
                                   ReportAgingAsOf date.
                                   One of: ReportEndDate, Today.
  --report-class-filter OBJECT     Allows you to query for a specified class.
                                   JSON: {"full_name", "full_name_with_children", …}. Exactly
                                   one of: list_id | full_name | list_id_with_children |
                                   full_name_with_children.
  --report-date-macro STRING       The time period covered by this report.
                                   (at most one of: --report-period | --report-date-macro)
                                   One of: All, LastMonth, LastMonthToDate, LastQuarter,
                                   LastQuarterToDate, LastWeek, LastWeekToDate, LastYear, … (24;
                                   --help --json lists them).
  --report-detail-level-filter STRING
                                   The level of detail to include in the report.
                                   One of: All, AllExceptSummary, SummaryOnly.
  --report-entity-filter OBJECT    Allows you to query for a specified name type (customer,
                                   employee, vendor, or other) or query for specific list
                                   elements.
                                   JSON: {"entity_type_filter", "full_name", …}. Exactly one of:
                                   entity_type_filter | list_id | full_name |
                                   list_id_with_children | full_name_with_children.
  --report-item-filter OBJECT      Allows you to query for a specified item type (for example,
                                   discount, inventory, or non-inventory) or query for specific
                                   list elements.
                                   JSON: {"full_name", "full_name_with_children", …}. Exactly
                                   one of: item_type_filter | list_id | full_name |
                                   list_id_with_children | full_name_with_children.
  --report-modified-date-range-filter OBJECT
                                   Returns reports that were modified between these two dates
                                   (inclusive).
                                   (at most one of: --report-modified-date-range-filter |
                                   --report-modified-date-range-macro)
                                   JSON: {"from_report_modified_date", …}
  --report-modified-date-range-macro STRING
                                   The time period covered by this report.
                                   (at most one of: --report-modified-date-range-filter |
                                   --report-modified-date-range-macro)
                                   One of: All, LastMonth, LastMonthToDate, LastQuarter,
                                   LastQuarterToDate, LastWeek, LastWeekToDate, LastYear, … (24;
                                   --help --json lists them).
  --report-period OBJECT           If you omit both ToReportDate and FromReportDate, the report
                                   will cover the current fiscal year to date.
                                   (at most one of: --report-period | --report-date-macro)
                                   JSON: {"from_report_date", "to_report_date"}
  --report-posting-status-filter STRING
                                   Allows you to query for posting reports, non-posting reports,
                                   or reports that are either one.
                                   One of: Either, NonPosting, Posting.
  --report-txn-type-filter OBJECT  Allows you to report on specific transaction types (for
                                   example, charge, check, deposit, or estimate).
                                   JSON: {"txn_type_filter"}. Required: txn_type_filter.
  --webhook-url URL                Where QuBe Sync POSTs the answered request (this same
                                   document with response_json filled in), signed with
                                   X-QUBE-Signature.

  --wait[=10m]  Wait here for QuickBooks' answer (every page of an iterated query) and print it.

--help --json prints the operation's full parameters and body schema, as JSON.

Example:
  qube qb aging-reports list <connection> --aging-report-type APAgingDetail

$ qube qb customers list --help
Usage: qube qb customers list <connection> [flags]

List customers.  GET /api/v2/connections/{connection_id}/customers

qbXML: CustomerQueryRq.

Returns data for the specified customers.

Important: We highly recommend that you use the IncludeRetElement tag in your CustomerQuery to
include any data you want but do NOT include the ShipAddress data in the Response, unless you
need to get the shipping address for a particular customer. Excluding the shipping address data
will significantly improve the performance of the CustomerQuery.

Flags:
  --request-id STRING            Your own label for the request, sent to QuickBooks as its qbXML
                                 requestID and echoed in the raw answer (response_xml);
                                 optional, since the answer's webhook already carries the
                                 request's id.
  --iterator                     Page through every result: QuBe Sync asks QuickBooks for each
                                 page in turn and sends each to your webhook_url as it arrives,
                                 all with the same id.
  --list-id STRING...            ListID and FullName both identify a list object.
                                 (at most one of: --list-id | --full-name | (--active-status,
                                 --from-modified-date, --max-returned, --name-contains,
                                 --name-ends-with, --name-range, --name-starts-with,
                                 --to-modified-date))
                                 (repeat the flag, or a JSON array)
  --full-name STRING...          FullName (along with ListID) is a way to identify a list
                                 object.
                                 (at most one of: --list-id | --full-name | (--active-status,
                                 --from-modified-date, --max-returned, --name-contains,
                                 --name-ends-with, --name-range, --name-starts-with,
                                 --to-modified-date))
                                 (repeat the flag, or a JSON array)
  --max-returned INTEGER         Limits the number of objects that a query returns.
                                 (not with --list-id or --full-name)
  --active-status STRING         Used in filters to select list objects based on whether or not
                                 they are currently enabled for use by QuickBooks.
                                 (not with --list-id or --full-name)
                                 One of: ActiveOnly, All, InactiveOnly.
  --from-modified-date DATETIME  Filters results to objects modified on or after this timestamp.
                                 (not with --list-id or --full-name)
  --to-modified-date DATETIME    Filters results to objects modified on or before this
                                 timestamp.
                                 (not with --list-id or --full-name)
  --name-starts-with STRING      Filters according to the object’s Name.
                                 (not with --list-id or --full-name; at most one of:
                                 (--name-contains, --name-ends-with, --name-starts-with) |
                                 --name-range)
  --name-contains STRING         Filters according to the object’s Name.
                                 (not with --list-id or --full-name; at most one of:
                                 (--name-contains, --name-ends-with, --name-starts-with) |
                                 --name-range)
  --name-ends-with STRING        Filters according to the object’s Name.
                                 (not with --list-id or --full-name; at most one of:
                                 (--name-contains, --name-ends-with, --name-starts-with) |
                                 --name-range)
  --name-range OBJECT            Filters according to the object’s Name.
                                 (not with --list-id or --full-name; at most one of:
                                 (--name-contains, --name-ends-with, --name-starts-with) |
                                 --name-range)
                                 JSON: {"from", "to"}
  --include STRING...            One of: AccountNumber, AdditionalContactRef,
                                 AdditionalNotesRet, AltContact, AltPhone, Balance, BillAddress,
                                 BillAddressBlock, … (63; --help --json lists them). (repeat the
                                 flag, or a JSON array)
  --owner-id STRING...           OwnerID identifies who owns the data extension. (repeat the
                                 flag, or a JSON array)
  --webhook-url URL              Where QuBe Sync POSTs the answered request (this same document
                                 with response_json filled in), signed with X-QUBE-Signature.

  --wait[=10m]  Wait here for QuickBooks' answer (every page of an iterated query) and print it.

--help --json prints the operation's full parameters and body schema, as JSON.

$ qube qb estimates update --help
Usage: qube qb estimates update <connection> [flags]

Update estimate.  PUT /api/v2/connections/{connection_id}/estimates

qbXML: EstimateModRq, from qbXML 3.0. The estimate_mod wrapper from qbXML is also accepted.

Some fields in an EstimateMod request can’t be cleared. If any of the following fields is
included in an estimate modify request, it must contain a value:

- CustomerRef
- TemplateRef
- TxnDate
- IsActive
- CreateChangeOrder
- DueDate
- ItemSalesTaxRef

Within EstimateLineMod or EstimateLineGroupMod:

- ItemRef
- Quantity
- Rate
- Amount>
- SalesTaxCodeRef
- Markup
(--help --json has the whole description.)

Flags:
  --webhook-url URL  Where QuBe Sync POSTs the answered request (this same document with
                     response_json filled in), signed with X-QUBE-Signature.

Body (JSON, required): --data JSON|@file|-, and/or its fields as flags (a flag wins):
  --edit-sequence STRING           (required) EditSequence is a server-generated revision token.
  --txn-id STRING                  (required) TxnID is the unique QuickBooks identifier for a
                                   transaction.
  --bill-address OBJECT            This aggregate supports at most five address lines.
                                   JSON: {"addr1", "city", "postal_code", "state", …}
  --class-ref OBJECT               Use classes to categorize transactions (for example, by
                                   department, location, or work type).
                                   JSON: {"full_name", "list_id"}
  --create-change-order            If CreateChangeOrder is true, the modifications are tracked
                                   as a “change order” that appears in the description field at
                                   the bottom of the estimate form in QuickBooks.
  --customer-msg-ref OBJECT        A standard message such as “Thank you for your business,” or
                                   “Please sign and return this estimate to indicate your
                                   approval.”
                                   JSON: {"full_name", "list_id"}
  --customer-ref OBJECT            CustomerRef identifies a customer or customer job.
                                   JSON: {"full_name", "list_id"}
  --customer-sales-tax-code-ref OBJECT
                                   Refers to the sales-tax code for sales related to this
                                   customer.
                                   JSON: {"full_name", "list_id"}
  --due-date DATE                  The date on which payment is due.
  --estimate-line-group-mod OBJECT...
                                   Represents a previously defined group of lines in the
                                   estimate.
                                   (can be combined with --estimate-line-mod)
                                   JSON: [{"txn_line_id", "estimate_line_mod", …}, ...]. In
                                   each, required: txn_line_id. (repeat the flag, or a JSON
                                   array)
  --estimate-line-mod OBJECT...    One line of this estimate.
                                   (can be combined with --estimate-line-group-mod)
                                   JSON: [{"txn_line_id", "amount", "class_ref", …}, ...]. In
                                   each, required: txn_line_id; at most one of: rate |
                                   rate_percent; at most one of: markup_rate |
                                   markup_rate_percent | price_level_ref. (repeat the flag, or a
                                   JSON array)
  --exchange-rate NUMBER           ExchangeRate is the rate from this currency to the company’s
                                   home currency at AsOfDate.
  --fob STRING                     QuickBooks uses the term FOB, “freight on board,” to indicate
                                   the place from which the product is shipped.
  --include-ret-element STRING...  One of: BillAddress, BillAddressBlock, ClassRef, CurrencyRef,
                                   CustomerMsgRef, CustomerRef, CustomerSalesTaxCodeRef,
                                   DataExtRet, … (41; --help --json lists them). (repeat the
                                   flag, or a JSON array)
  --is-active                      If IsActive is true, this object is currently enabled for use
                                   by QuickBooks.
  --is-tax-included                For future use with international versions of QuickBooks.
  --is-to-be-emailed               When enabled, QuickBooks validates the customer email at
                                   runtime before sending.
  --item-sales-tax-ref OBJECT      An ItemSalesTax entry represents one tax rate paid to one tax
                                   agency.
                                   JSON: {"full_name", "list_id"}
  --memo STRING                    Message that appears in reports, but not on the estimate.
  --other STRING                   Other, Other1, and Other2 are built-in transaction custom
                                   fields.
  --po-number STRING               Purchase order number.
  --ref-number STRING              RefNumber is a user-managed transaction reference (for
                                   example, a check number).
  --request-id STRING              Your own label for the request, sent to QuickBooks as its
                                   qbXML requestID and echoed in the raw answer (response_xml);
                                   optional, since the answer's webhook already carries the
                                   request's id.
  --sales-rep-ref OBJECT           A sales representative must be on the Employee, Vendor, or
                                   Other Names list within QuickBooks.
                                   JSON: {"full_name", "list_id"}
  --ship-address OBJECT            This aggregate supports at most five address lines.
                                   JSON: {"addr1", "addr2", "addr3", "addr4", "addr5", …}
  --template-ref OBJECT            QuickBooks templates specify how to print certain
                                   transactions.
                                   JSON: {"full_name", "list_id"}
  --terms-ref OBJECT               Refers to the payment terms associated with this entity.
                                   JSON: {"full_name", "list_id"}
  --txn-date DATE                  The date of the transaction.

  --wait[=10m]  Wait here for QuickBooks' answer (every page of an iterated query) and print it.
  --example     Print the example body below as JSON, to edit and send with --data @file.

--help --json prints the operation's full parameters and body schema, as JSON.

Example:
  qube qb estimates update <connection> --data '{"bill_address":{…},"customer_ref":{…},"edit_sequence":"1700000000",…,"txn_id":"1A2B-1700000000"}'

  That body is shortened: {…} and […] stand for more. The whole of it, to edit and send:
  qube qb estimates update --example > estimate.json
  qube qb estimates update <connection> --data @estimate.json

$ qube qb estimates update --example
{
  "bill_address": {
    "addr1": "123 Main St",
    "city": "Springfield",
    "postal_code": "62701",
    "state": "IL"
  },
  "customer_ref": {
    "full_name": "Acme Corp"
  },
  "edit_sequence": "1700000000",
  "memo": "Monthly service",
  "ref_number": "1001",
  "txn_date": "2026-01-15",
  "txn_id": "1A2B-1700000000"
}

$ qube qb invoices create --help
Usage: qube qb invoices create <connection> [flags]

Create invoice.  POST /api/v2/connections/{connection_id}/invoices

qbXML: InvoiceAddRq. The invoice_add wrapper from qbXML is also accepted.

An invoice records the amount owed by a customer who purchased goods or services but didn’t pay
in full at the time of the sale. If full payment is received at the time of the sale, it is
recorded as a sales receipt, not an invoice.

In QuickBooks, invoices and estimates use similar fields, and an estimate can be converted into
an invoice after the customer accepts the estimate. However, in the SDK, there is currently no
ability to create an invoice directly from an estimate (you can’t link an invoice to an
estimate).
(--help --json has the whole description.)

Flags:
  --webhook-url URL  Where QuBe Sync POSTs the answered request (this same document with
                     response_json filled in), signed with X-QUBE-Signature.

Body (JSON, required): --data JSON|@file|-, and/or its fields as flags (a flag wins):
  --customer-ref OBJECT            (required) CustomerRef identifies a customer or customer job.
                                   JSON: {"full_name", "list_id"}
  --ar-account-ref OBJECT          ARAccountRef points to an Accounts Receivable account.
                                   JSON: {"full_name", "list_id"}
  --bill-address OBJECT            This aggregate supports at most five address lines.
                                   JSON: {"addr1", "city", "postal_code", "state", …}
  --class-ref OBJECT               Use classes to categorize transactions (for example, by
                                   department, location, or work type).
                                   JSON: {"full_name", "list_id"}
  --customer-msg-ref OBJECT        A standard message such as “Thank you for your business,” or
                                   “Please sign and return this estimate to indicate your
                                   approval.”
                                   JSON: {"full_name", "list_id"}
  --customer-sales-tax-code-ref OBJECT
                                   Refers to the sales-tax code for sales related to this
                                   customer.
                                   JSON: {"full_name", "list_id"}
  --def-macro STRING               defMacro lets you assign a reusable name to a returned TxnID
                                   or TxnLineID so later requests can reference it with
                                   useMacro.
  --discount-line-add OBJECT       JSON: {"account_ref", "amount", "is_taxable", …}. At most one
                                   of: amount | rate_percent.
  --due-date DATE                  If DueDate isn’t included in an InvoiceAdd request,
                                   QuickBooks might determine the due date according to the
                                   terms set for this customer.
  --exchange-rate NUMBER           ExchangeRate is the rate from this currency to the company’s
                                   home currency at AsOfDate.
  --external-guid STRING           Allows for the attachment of a user defined GUID value.
  --fob STRING                     QuickBooks uses the term FOB, “freight on board,” to indicate
                                   the place from which the product is shipped.
  --include-ret-element STRING...  One of: ARAccountRef, AppliedAmount, BalanceRemaining,
                                   BalanceRemainingInHomeCurrency, BillAddress,
                                   BillAddressBlock, ClassRef, CurrencyRef, … (53; --help --json
                                   lists them). (repeat the flag, or a JSON array)
  --invoice-line-add OBJECT...     Represents one line in the invoice.
                                   (can be combined with --invoice-line-group-add)
                                   JSON: [{"desc", "item_ref", "quantity", "rate", …}, ...]. In
                                   each, at most one of: rate | rate_percent | price_level_ref;
                                   at most one of: serial_number | lot_number. (repeat the flag,
                                   or a JSON array)
  --invoice-line-group-add OBJECT...
                                   Represents a previously defined group of lines in the
                                   invoice.
                                   (can be combined with --invoice-line-add)
                                   JSON: [{"item_group_ref", "data_ext", "desc", …}, ...]. In
                                   each, required: item_group_ref. (repeat the flag, or a JSON
                                   array)
  --is-finance-charge              If IsFinanceCharge is true, a finance-charge item has been
                                   added to the invoice.
  --is-pending                     If IsPending is set to true, the invoice hasn’t been
                                   completed.
  --is-tax-included                For future use with international versions of QuickBooks.
  --is-to-be-emailed               When enabled, QuickBooks validates the customer email at
                                   runtime before sending.
  --is-to-be-printed               If IsToBePrinted is true, QuickBooks queues the form for
                                   later printing.
  --item-sales-tax-ref OBJECT      An ItemSalesTax entry represents one tax rate paid to one tax
                                   agency.
                                   JSON: {"full_name", "list_id"}
  --link-to-txn-id STRING...       (repeat the flag, or a JSON array)
  --memo STRING                    Message that appears in reports, but not on the invoice.
  --other STRING                   Other, Other1, and Other2 are built-in transaction custom
                                   fields.
  --po-number STRING               Purchase order number.
  --ref-number STRING              RefNumber is a user-managed transaction reference (for
                                   example, a check number).
  --request-id STRING              Your own label for the request, sent to QuickBooks as its
                                   qbXML requestID and echoed in the raw answer (response_xml);
                                   optional, since the answer's webhook already carries the
                                   request's id.
  --sales-rep-ref OBJECT           A sales representative must be on the Employee, Vendor, or
                                   Other Names list within QuickBooks.
                                   JSON: {"full_name", "list_id"}
  --sales-tax-line-add OBJECT      JSON: {"account_ref", "amount", "rate_percent"}. At most one
                                   of: amount | rate_percent.
  --set-credit OBJECT...           JSON: [{"applied_amount", "credit_txn_id", …}, ...]. In each,
                                   required: applied_amount, credit_txn_id. (repeat the flag, or
                                   a JSON array)
  --ship-address OBJECT            This aggregate supports at most five address lines.
                                   JSON: {"addr1", "addr2", "addr3", "addr4", "addr5", …}
  --ship-date DATE                 The date when the product was, or will be, shipped.
  --ship-method-ref OBJECT         A shipping method, for example, standard mail or overnight
                                   delivery.
                                   JSON: {"full_name", "list_id"}
  --shipping-line-add OBJECT       JSON: {"amount", "account_ref"}. Required: amount.
  --template-ref OBJECT            QuickBooks templates specify how to print certain
                                   transactions.
                                   JSON: {"full_name", "list_id"}
  --terms-ref OBJECT               Refers to the payment terms associated with this entity.
                                   JSON: {"full_name", "list_id"}
  --txn-date DATE                  The date of the invoice.

  --wait[=10m]  Wait here for QuickBooks' answer (every page of an iterated query) and print it.
  --example     Print the example body below as JSON, to edit and send with --data @file.

--help --json prints the operation's full parameters and body schema, as JSON.

Example:
  qube qb invoices create <connection> --data '{"bill_address":{…},"customer_ref":{…},"invoice_line_add":[…],"memo":"Monthly service",…}'

  That body is shortened: {…} and […] stand for more. The whole of it, to edit and send:
  qube qb invoices create --example > invoice.json
  qube qb invoices create <connection> --data @invoice.json

$ qube qb invoices create --example
{
  "bill_address": {
    "addr1": "123 Main St",
    "city": "Springfield",
    "postal_code": "62701",
    "state": "IL"
  },
  "customer_ref": {
    "full_name": "Acme Corp"
  },
  "invoice_line_add": [
    {
      "desc": "Blue widget, large",
      "item_ref": {
        "full_name": "Widget"
      },
      "quantity": 2,
      "rate": 125.0
    }
  ],
  "memo": "Monthly service",
  "ref_number": "1001",
  "txn_date": "2026-01-15"
}

$ qube qb journal-entries create --help
Usage: qube qb journal-entries create <connection> [flags]

Create journal entry.  POST /api/v2/connections/{connection_id}/journal-entries

qbXML: JournalEntryAddRq. The journal_entry_add wrapper from qbXML is also accepted.

In versions 1.0 and 1.1, all debit lines in a JournalEntryAdd request had to appear first,
followed by all credit lines. But in versions 2.0 and above, the debit and credit lines can be
intermingled. A credit line can legally come first in thejournal entry add. In traditional
accounting, transactions are entered into the general journal and categorized exclusively by
account. In QuickBooks, most transactions can be categorized either by account or by type
(invoice, check, and so on). For a few activities in QuickBooks, you must use the general
journal directly, for example for recording depreciation. …
(--help --json has the whole description.)

Flags:
  --webhook-url URL  Where QuBe Sync POSTs the answered request (this same document with
                     response_json filled in), signed with X-QUBE-Signature.

Body (JSON, required): --data JSON|@file|-, and/or its fields as flags (a flag wins):
  --currency-ref OBJECT            A currency record defines how QuickBooks stores and displays
                                   a currency.
                                   (at most one of: --is-home-currency-adjustment |
                                   (--currency-ref, --exchange-rate,
                                   --is-amounts-entered-in-home-currency))
                                   JSON: {"full_name", "list_id"}
  --def-macro STRING               defMacro lets you assign a reusable name to a returned TxnID
                                   or TxnLineID so later requests can reference it with
                                   useMacro.
  --exchange-rate NUMBER           ExchangeRate is the rate from this currency to the company’s
                                   home currency at AsOfDate.
                                   (at most one of: --is-home-currency-adjustment |
                                   (--currency-ref, --exchange-rate,
                                   --is-amounts-entered-in-home-currency))
  --external-guid STRING           Allows for the attachment of a user defined GUID value.
  --include-ret-element STRING...  One of: CurrencyRef, DataExtRet, EditSequence, ExchangeRate,
                                   ExternalGUID, IsAdjustment, IsAmountsEnteredInHomeCurrency,
                                   IsHomeCurrencyAdjustment, … (19; --help --json lists them).
                                   (repeat the flag, or a JSON array)
  --is-adjustment                  When a change is an “adjustment,” the original information is
                                   retained, usually so an accountant can see the effects of the
                                   adjustment.
  --is-amounts-entered-in-home-currency
                                   Flag that indicates the amount being entered is in the home
                                   currency.
                                   (at most one of: --is-home-currency-adjustment |
                                   (--currency-ref, --exchange-rate,
                                   --is-amounts-entered-in-home-currency))
  --is-home-currency-adjustment    Indicates whether the transaction is using a home currency or
                                   foreign currency.
                                   (at most one of: --is-home-currency-adjustment |
                                   (--currency-ref, --exchange-rate,
                                   --is-amounts-entered-in-home-currency))
  --journal-credit-line OBJECT...  If no amount is included in the first credit line (or debit
                                   line) of a journal entry in the QuickBooks user interface,
                                   then subsequent SDK queries on that JournalEntry object will
                                   fail XML validation.
                                   (can be combined with --journal-debit-line; at least one
                                   entry is required across them)
                                   JSON: [{"account_ref", "amount", "memo", …}, ...] (repeat the
                                   flag, or a JSON array)
  --journal-debit-line OBJECT...   If no amount is included in the first debit line (or credit
                                   line) of a journal entry in the QuickBooks user interface,
                                   then subsequent SDK queries on that JournalEntry object will
                                   fail XML validation.
                                   (can be combined with --journal-credit-line; at least one
                                   entry is required across them)
                                   JSON: [{"account_ref", "amount", "memo", …}, ...] (repeat the
                                   flag, or a JSON array)
  --memo STRING                    Additional information.
  --ref-number STRING              RefNumber is a user-managed transaction reference (for
                                   example, a check number).
  --request-id STRING              Your own label for the request, sent to QuickBooks as its
                                   qbXML requestID and echoed in the raw answer (response_xml);
                                   optional, since the answer's webhook already carries the
                                   request's id.
  --txn-date DATE                  The date of the transaction.

  --wait[=10m]  Wait here for QuickBooks' answer (every page of an iterated query) and print it.
  --example     Print the example body below as JSON, to edit and send with --data @file.

--help --json prints the operation's full parameters and body schema, as JSON.

Example:
  qube qb journal-entries create <connection> --data '{"journal_credit_line":[…],"journal_debit_line":[…],"memo":"Monthly service","ref_number":"1001",…}'

  That body is shortened: {…} and […] stand for more. The whole of it, to edit and send:
  qube qb journal-entries create --example > journal-entry.json
  qube qb journal-entries create <connection> --data @journal-entry.json

$ qube qb journal-entries create --example
{
  "journal_credit_line": [
    {
      "account_ref": {
        "full_name": "Checking"
      },
      "amount": 250.0,
      "memo": "Monthly service"
    }
  ],
  "journal_debit_line": [
    {
      "account_ref": {
        "full_name": "Office Supplies"
      },
      "amount": 250.0,
      "memo": "Monthly service"
    }
  ],
  "memo": "Monthly service",
  "ref_number": "1001",
  "txn_date": "2026-01-15"
}

$ qube qb vehicle-mileages create --help
Usage: qube qb vehicle-mileages create <connection> [flags]

Create vehicle mileage.  POST /api/v2/connections/{connection_id}/vehicle-mileages

qbXML: VehicleMileageAddRq, from qbXML 6.0. The vehicle_mileage_add wrapper from qbXML is also
accepted.

Adds a VehicleMileage transaction involving the specified vehicle for the specified total miles
or optionally for the QuickBooks calculated total miles if you supply the odometer start and
end. Notice that only dates are supported for vehicle mileage, so make sure you enter any
datetime values with dates only, no time values. That is, 2002-02-02 will work for trip end date
or start date, but 2002-02-02T12:20:20 won’t work. …
(--help --json has the whole description.)

Flags:
  --webhook-url URL  Where QuBe Sync POSTs the answered request (this same document with
                     response_json filled in), signed with X-QUBE-Signature.

Body (JSON, required): --data JSON|@file|-, and/or its fields as flags (a flag wins):
  --vehicle-ref OBJECT             (required) A vehicle for use in VehicleMileage transactions.
                                   JSON: {"full_name", "list_id"}
  --billable-status STRING         The billing status of this item line or expense line.
                                   One of: Billable, HasBeenBilled, NotBillable.
  --class-ref OBJECT               Use classes to categorize transactions (for example, by
                                   department, location, or work type).
                                   JSON: {"full_name", "list_id"}
  --customer-ref OBJECT            CustomerRef identifies a customer or customer job.
                                   JSON: {"full_name", "list_id"}
  --def-macro STRING               defMacro lets you assign a reusable name to a returned TxnID
                                   or TxnLineID so later requests can reference it with
                                   useMacro.
  --include-ret-element STRING...  One of: BillableAmount, BillableRate, BillableStatus,
                                   ClassRef, CustomerRef, EditSequence, ExternalGUID, ItemRef, …
                                   (23; --help --json lists them). (repeat the flag, or a JSON
                                   array)
  --item-ref OBJECT                ItemRef may accept all item types or a restricted subset,
                                   depending on the request.
                                   JSON: {"full_name", "list_id"}
  --notes STRING                   Additional information.
  --odometer-end NUMBER            Odometer reading at the end of the trip.
                                   (exactly one of: (--odometer-end, --odometer-start) |
                                   --total-miles)
  --odometer-start NUMBER          Odometer reading at the start of the trip.
                                   (exactly one of: (--odometer-end, --odometer-start) |
                                   --total-miles)
  --request-id STRING              Your own label for the request, sent to QuickBooks as its
                                   qbXML requestID and echoed in the raw answer (response_xml);
                                   optional, since the answer's webhook already carries the
                                   request's id.
  --total-miles NUMBER             Total trip miles.
                                   (exactly one of: (--odometer-end, --odometer-start) |
                                   --total-miles)
  --trip-end-date DATE             Date the trip ended.
  --trip-start-date DATE           Date the trip began.

  --wait[=10m]  Wait here for QuickBooks' answer (every page of an iterated query) and print it.
  --example     Print the example body below as JSON, to edit and send with --data @file.

--help --json prints the operation's full parameters and body schema, as JSON.

Example:
  qube qb vehicle-mileages create <connection> --data '{"customer_ref":{…},"item_ref":{…},"odometer_end":12045,"odometer_start":12000,"vehicle_ref":{…}}'

  That body is shortened: {…} and […] stand for more. The whole of it, to edit and send:
  qube qb vehicle-mileages create --example > vehicle-mileage.json
  qube qb vehicle-mileages create <connection> --data @vehicle-mileage.json

$ qube qb vehicle-mileages create --example
{
  "customer_ref": {
    "full_name": "Acme Corp"
  },
  "item_ref": {
    "full_name": "Mileage"
  },
  "odometer_end": 12045,
  "odometer_start": 12000,
  "vehicle_ref": {
    "full_name": "Delivery Van"
  }
}

